1. Summary of the EDA completed so far
The merged dataset contains 13,584 rows and 74 columns, covering economic data from 1960–2025. The notebook identifies 2,715 Olympic observations spanning 1960–2016, with 1,824 observations in the 1990–2016 analysis window.

The cleaning process removed eight economic indicators with less than 10% coverage, leaving 54 economic indicators. Missingness remains substantial: 15 retained indicators are missing for more than half of the 1990–2016 Olympic panel. Additionally, 54 Olympic observations have no retained economic data or WDI country code.

The main findings are:

Medals are highly unevenly distributed. Only 39% of Olympic observations have any medals; the median medal count is zero, while the maximum is 217. Models must handle both many zeros and a small number of dominant countries.
Participation strongly relates to medals. Athlete count has a Pearson correlation of approximately 0.887 with medals in the 1990+ observations.
National economic size appears important. In the eligible 2016 modeling sample, total GDP has a correlation of 0.864 with medals, compared with 0.394 for population and 0.186 for GDP per capita.
Other indicators show useful preliminary associations. Tourism arrivals, private-sector credit, health expenditure, internet access, and life expectancy are positively correlated with medals in the 2016 sample.
Missingness differs across countries and indicators. This could affect which countries the models represent well.
GDP/population grouping creates small subgroups. These groups are useful for descriptive comparisons, but fitting separate models within them reduces the available training data.
These correlations are descriptive associations, not evidence that changing an indicator would cause a country to win more medals.

